     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter **amlodipine 50 mg daily**, and I want to explain why rather than just block you.\n\nThere's no accepted use for 50 mg of amlodipine. The maximum is 10 mg a day, and higher doses don't lower blood pressure further. They just raise the risk of severe hypotension and shock. Because amlodipine stays in the body so long, that toxicity can last for days. Your confirmation reads like a reply to my question rather than a clinical reason, and a mistyped \"5\" is one of the most common ordering errors. So I don't think the risk to the patient is worth it here.\n\nHere's what I can do right now:\n- **Enter 5 mg by mouth once daily**, or any dose from 2.5 to 10 mg you choose.\n- **Enter 10 mg once daily**, the maximum, if you want the strongest effect amlodipine can give.\n\nIf 50 mg really is intended, such as for a research protocol or something I'm not aware of, please discuss it directly with the pharmacist. They can check the reasoning and enter or approve the order through the usual high-alert process. I'd also suggest checking that the order is for the right patient and the right drug.\n\nShould I go ahead with 5 mg?"
      }
